A||||0||MrPBody||Porting the Duke II -- the adventure continues||||1||
Z||000000||MrPBody||02-24-2001||09:37 PM||||Thanks to all who provided advice on porting my 2.5.  I ignored some of your warnings, because I have to learn the hard way.<P>I bought a rebuilt head because my spare was cracked, and I think the head now on the car is cracked, too.  After porting (see below), I expect to assemble and install the head within the next month.<P>Here are before-and-after porting photos:<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/portbef01.jpg"> <BR><B>Intake port - before</B><BR>Port is slightly trapezoidal and noticeably smaller than the gasket opening.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/portaft01.jpg"> <BR><B>Intake port - after</B><BR>Port enlargement extends only a little way (<I>ca.</I> 1/2 inch) into the tapered passage.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/valvebef01.jpg"> <BR><B>Valve guides - before</B><BR>The intake passage describes a 3-dimensional spiral around the near-cylindrical valve guide boss, the end of which is machined flat.  Note the cast ridges in the passage "tail".<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/valveaft01.jpg"> <BR><B>Valve guides - after</B><BR>Intake valve guide boss is tapered and rounded.  Intake passage shape and surface is otherwise unchanged.  Exhaust guide boss is slightly rounded.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seatprot01.jpg"> <BR><B>Valve seat protector</B><BR>A plastic strip cut from the icing cup in Pillsbury breakfast rolls.  I also wrapped the Dremel chuck in electrical tape.<P><BR>In a couple of the above photos, you may faintly see where the head was welded and re-machined around the exhaust valve seats.<P>My porting goal was to improve flow without badly dorking-up GM's econo design.  The results don't look shiny and elegant, but we'll see how this runs in a few weeks.  Stay tuned!<BR>||||reg||1
